{"assets":[],"contentKind":"statement","contestId":"1907","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"attrs":{"level":1,"role":"title"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"E. Good Triples"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"role":"time_limit"},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"time limit per test"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":"2 seconds"}],"kind":"container"},{"attrs":{"role":"memory_limit"},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"memory limit per test"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":"256 megabytes"}],"kind":"container"},{"attrs":{"role":"body"},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Given a non-negative integer number $$$n$$$ ($$$n \\ge 0$$$). Let's say a triple of non-negative integers $$$(a, b, c)$$$ is good if $$$a + b + c = n$$$, and $$$digsum(a) + digsum(b) + digsum(c) = digsum(n)$$$, where $$$digsum(x)$$$ is the sum of digits of number $$$x$$$."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"For example, if $$$n = 26$$$, then the pair $$$(4, 12, 10)$$$ is good, because $$$4 + 12 + 10 = 26$$$, and $$$(4) + (1 + 2) + (1 + 0) = (2 + 6)$$$."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Your task is to find the number of good triples for the given number $$$n$$$. The order of the numbers in a triple matters. For example, the triples $$$(4, 12, 10)$$$ and $$$(10, 12, 4)$$$ are two different triples."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"input_specification"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Input"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The first line of input contains a single integer $$$t$$$ ($$$1 \\le t \\le 10^4$$$) — the number of test cases. Descriptions of test cases follow."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The first and only line of the test case contains one integer $$$n$$$ ($$$0 \\le n \\le 10^7$$$)."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"output_specification"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Output"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"For each test case output one integer, the number of good triples for the given integer $$$n$$$. Order of integers in a triple matters."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"samples"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Example"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"role":"sample"},"children":[{"attrs":{"role":"sample_input"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Input"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"\n12\n11\n0\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n3141\n999\n2718\n9999999\n10000000\n"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"sample_output"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Output"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"\n9\n1\n3\n6\n10\n15\n21\n1350\n166375\n29160\n1522435234375\n3\n"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"note"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Note"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In the first example, the good triples are $$$(0, 0, 11)$$$, $$$(0, 1, 10)$$$, $$$(0, 10, 1)$$$, $$$(0, 11, 0)$$$, $$$(1, 0, 10)$$$, $$$(1, 10, 0)$$$, $$$(10, 0, 1)$$$, $$$(10, 1, 0)$$$, $$$(11, 0, 0)$$$."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In the second example, there is only one good triple $$$(0, 0, 0)$$$."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"document"},"index":"E","problemCode":"1907E","schema":2,"sourceKind":"html","sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/contest/1907/problem/E"}